#Base plane frames for the stacking term.
#
#Format: <resname> <atom0> <atom1> <atom2>
#
#Three atoms of one nucleobase that span its plane. The stacking term takes the
#base's CENTRE as their centroid and its NORMAL as (atom1-atom0) x (atom2-atom0);
#both factors of the term are even in the normal, so the order the three are
#named in cannot change any energy or force.
#
#ATOMS ARE NAMED BY THEIR GROUP, like DNADonorAcceptor.hbond and for the same
#reason: that is the name the topology carries once it has been reduced.
#
#WHICH THREE, AND WHY. Chosen by measurement over the 4757 complete nucleobases
#of 8PKL -- an RNA structure, which is what carries a large verified sample of
#all four ring systems. Thymine is not in it and inherits uracil's frame: the
#two rings differ only by the C5 methyl, which C6-C4-C2 does not touch.
#
#Ranking every triple of ring atoms on three things: how far from
#collinear it is (the normal's conditioning), how far its centroid sits from the
#centroid of ALL that base's ring atoms, and how far its normal tilts from the
#ring's best-fit normal.
#
#  purines, C8-C6-N3    sin of the smallest angle 0.74 (A) / 0.75 (G)
#                       centroid error 0.151 A (A) / 0.157 A (G)
#                       tilt 0.028 deg
#  pyrimidines, C6-C4-C2  sin 0.853 (C) / 0.857 (U)
#                       centroid error 0.014 A (C) / 0.024 A (U)
#                       tilt 0.06 deg
#
#The centroid error is SYSTEMATIC, not noisy -- its maximum over 4757 bases
#equals its mean to within 0.001 A, because a nucleobase ring is rigid -- so it
#shifts the equilibrium distance by a fixed amount and is absorbed by r_st
#rather than blurring the well. For the pyrimidines two triples tie
#(N1-C5-N3 scores identically); C6-C4-C2 is taken because all three are ring
#CARBONS, which keeps the frame off both the glycosidic nitrogen and the
#Watson-Crick N3, so the plane is measured from atoms the pairing term does not
#also use.
#
#Companion to DNADonorAcceptor.hbond, DNAAtomRigidGroups.rbody and
#DNAAtomTorsion.bi.ff, and named like them.


DA   aC8  aC6  aN3
DG   gC8  gC6  gN3
DC   cC6  cC4  cC2
DT   tC6  tC4  tC2
